  "query_preview": "..While an increase in moisture is noted across the\ncentral and southern portions of Utah this afternoon, the coverage\nof showers and thunderstorms is rather dismal as originally\nanticipated. That said, it is evident that the environment is\nconducive to thunderstorms and heavy rainfall as SPC mesoanalysis\nsuggests upwards of 1500-2000J/kg of CAPE paired with PWAT values\naround 1 inch in southern Utah. One singular storm moving across\nthe southern area has been able to tap into this instability and\nmaintain itself while the remainder of the thunderstorm activity\nhas been rather short-lived. In any case, moisture has positioned\nitself in place across the southern half of Utah and will\ncontinue to increase subtly over the next couple of days. \n\nCoverage of showers and thunderstorms is anticipated to be just as\nisolated on Saturday as weak subsidence in the wake of the current\nshortwave inhibits widespread thunderstorm activity. The more\nnoteworthy widespread weather that Saturday will feature will be\nan increase in southwest winds across much of the region, with the\nstrongest winds targeting west-central and southwest Utah, as well\nas southwest Wyoming. In these areas, afternoon wind gusts will\nreach upwards of 30-35 mph as a trough remains over the PacNW\nregion. This trough is slated to progress inland and graze\nnorthern Utah through the second half of Sunday and into Monday\nmorning, introducing a cold front to the northern area. Behind the\ncold front, a brief period of stronger west to northwest winds can\nbe expected in northern Utah on the lee sides of mountain ranges.\n\nCooler temperatures are expected in the wake of the cold front on\nMonday across the northern area, with warm southerly return flow \nbringing a gradual warming trend into the middle of the week. For\nsouthern Utah, some level of moisture will remain in place from\nnow through next week, bringing at least a chance for showers and\nthunderstorms each afternoon.",
